.TH "LMM" "1" "Oct 2026" "lmm 2.1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
lmm-service-install - Install, enable and start lmm-serve.service for this lmm binary


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlmm service install [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Write lmm-serve.service to the directory the systemd user manager loads
units from ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user as the user manager sees it,
default ~/.config/systemd/user) with ExecStart pointing at this lmm binary,
then reload the user manager and enable and start the service.

.PP
The service reads the same config and data as this command: --config and
--data are passed on to it, and so are XDG_CONFIG_HOME and XDG_DATA_HOME
when they differ from the user manager's. It runs with the user manager's
PATH, not your shell's (see: systemctl --user show-environment), so 7z,
unrar and steamcmd must be on that one.

.PP
Running it again rewrites the unit (say, with a new --addr) and restarts the
service. If a package already installed the unit, nothing is written: lmm
tells you the systemctl command that turns it on. A unit file lmm did not
write is left alone.

.PP
Examples:
  lmm service install
  lmm service install --addr 127.0.0.1:8080


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--addr\fP=""
	address 'lmm serve' listens on (host:port; default 127.0.0.1:7420)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for install


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config directory (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lmm or ~/.config/lmm)

.PP
\fB--data\fP=""
	data directory (default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/lmm or ~/.local/share/lmm)

.PP
\fB-g\fP, \fB--game\fP=""
	game ID to operate on

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output JSON instead of text; mutating commands print their result, --dry-run prints the plan; never prompts

.PP
\fB--log-level\fP="off"
	diagnostic log level written to stderr (off, error, warn, info, debug)

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	disable colored output (NO_COLOR env is also honored)

.PP
\fB--no-hooks\fP[=false]
	disable all hooks

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


